.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0 WITH LLVM-exception
.Dd November 20, 2026
.Dt NQCMSSIGN 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqcmssign ,
.Nm nqcmsvrfy ,
.Nm nqcmsenc ,
.Nm nqcmsdec
.Nd the Cryptographic Message Syntax: signed and encrypted messages
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fn nqcmssign "nqsalg alg" "const uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen" "const uint8_t *cert" "size_t certlen" "const uint8_t *msg" "size_t n" "int detached" "int64_t at" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Ft int
.Fn nqcmsvrfy "const uint8_t *cms" "size_t n" "const uint8_t *msg" "size_t mlen" "const uint8_t *const *anc" "const size_t *anclen" "size_t nanc" "int64_t at" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen" "nqx5f *signer"
.Ft int
.Fn nqcmsenc "const uint8_t *const *rcert" "const size_t *rcertlen" "size_t nr" "const uint8_t *msg" "size_t n" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Ft int
.Fn nqcmsdec "nqkalg alg" "const uint8_t *sk" "size_t sklen" "const uint8_t *cert" "size_t certlen" "const uint8_t *cms" "size_t n" "uint8_t *out" "size_t cap" "size_t *outlen"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These functions write and read the Cryptographic Message Syntax (RFC
5652), in DER, with the certificates of
.Xr nqx5self 3 .
.Pp
.Fn nqcmssign
signs the
.Fa n
bytes at
.Fa msg
with the secret key
.Fa sk
of
.Fa alg ,
whose certificate is
.Fa cert ,
at the time
.Fa at :
a SignedData of one signer, with the signed attributes content type,
signing time and message digest, the signature over them (RFC 9882 for
ML-DSA, RFC 9814 for SLH-DSA, RFC 8419 for Ed25519), and the
certificate inside.
The content is inside too, or left out when
.Fa detached
is not 0.
.Pp
.Fn nqcmsvrfy
checks a SignedData of one signer: the digest of the content, the
signed attributes, the signature under the signer's certificate, and
that certificate against the
.Fa nanc
trust anchors
.Fa anc
(certificates in DER): it is one of them, or chains to one through the
certificates the message carries.
The content is the one inside, or
.Fa msg
for a detached signature; given both, they must be equal.
The certificates must hold at the time
.Fa at ,
or with
.Fa at
0 at the signing time the message states, else now.
On success the content is copied to
.Fa out
when that is not
.Dv NULL ,
and
.Fa signer ,
when not
.Dv NULL ,
says who signed
.Pq Xr nqx5self 3 .
Signatures by RSA and ECDSA certificates verify as well.
.Pp
.Fn nqcmsenc
encrypts
.Fa msg
to the
.Fa nr
recipients (at most 32) whose ML-KEM certificates are given: an
AuthEnvelopedData (RFC 5083) under AES-256-GCM (RFC 5084), the content
key carried to each recipient in a KEMRecipientInfo (RFC 9629, RFC
9936: ML-KEM, HKDF with SHA-256, AES Key Wrap).
.Fn nqcmsdec
opens one with the ML-KEM secret key
.Fa sk
of
.Fa alg ;
.Fa cert ,
when not
.Dv NULL ,
is that key's certificate and picks its entry among the recipients,
else every entry of that algorithm is tried.
.Ss Constructions
A signature is over the DER of the signed attributes, tagged as a SET
OF (RFC 5652 section 5.4): the content type, the signing time and the
message digest H(content), with H SHA-512 for ML-DSA (RFC 9882) and
Ed25519 (RFC 8419), and for SLH-DSA the hash of its parameter set
(RFC 9814): SHA-256 for SLH-DSA-SHA2-128s and -128f, SHA-512 for the
other SHA2 sets, SHAKE128 (32 bytes) for SLH-DSA-SHAKE-128s and -128f,
SHAKE256 (64 bytes) for the other SHAKE sets.
ML-DSA and SLH-DSA sign in their pure form with the empty context.
.Pp
An encrypted message is, with
.Sy ||
concatenation, CEK 32 random bytes, N 12 random bytes and, for each
recipient, ek the ML-KEM public key of its certificate:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(c, ss) = ML-KEM.Encaps(ek)                         FIPS 203
L       = 16 for ML-KEM-512, 32 for ML-KEM-768 and -1024
OI      = DER(CMSORIforKEMOtherInfo { wrap, kekLength = L })
            wrap = id-aes128-wrap (L = 16) or id-aes256-wrap (L = 32),
            no ukm
KEK     = HKDF-SHA-256(salt = "", IKM = ss, info = OI, L)
EK      = AES-KW(KEK, CEK)                          RFC 3394, 40 bytes
(C, T)  = AES-256-GCM(CEK, N, aad = "", content)    16-byte tag T
.Ed
.Pp
Each recipient gets a KEMRecipientInfo (RFC 9629 section 3) with its
issuer and serial number, the KEM, c, the KDF id-alg-hkdf-with-sha256,
L, wrap and EK; the AuthEnvelopedData carries N and the tag length 16 in
its GCMParameters (RFC 5084), C and T.
.Fn nqcmsdec
also takes AES-128-GCM, tags of 12 to 16 bytes and authenticated
attributes, which then are the associated data (RFC 5083 section 2.2).
.Pp
Encryption does not say who sent a message: to do both, sign first and
encrypt the result.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Dv NQOK
on success.
.Dv NQEVRFY
when a signature, a digest or a certificate path does not check out,
when no recipient entry opens with the key, or when the content does
not authenticate: nothing is given out then.
.Dv NQEARG
for a
.Dv NULL
pointer, a key that is not the certificate's, a certificate of the
wrong kind of key, or a detached signature with no content given;
.Dv NQEDEC
for a message or certificate that is not one;
.Dv NQENOSUP
for an algorithm these functions do not take, or more than one signer;
.Dv NQEBUF
when
.Fa out
is too small, with the size in
.Fa outlen ;
.Dv NQELIM
over 1 GiB.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-smime 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqerr 3 ,
.Xr nqsmsign 3 ,
.Xr nqx5self 3
.Sh STANDARDS
RFC 5652, RFC 5083, RFC 5084, RFC 8419, RFC 9629, RFC 9814, RFC 9882,
RFC 9936, RFC 3394 and RFC 5869.
